---
import { Code } from "astro:components";

import ComponentPage from "../../layouts/ComponentPage.astro";
import BadgeDemo from "../../components/BadgeDemo";
import { codeTheme } from "../../components/code-theme";

const usageExample = `import { Badge } from "@/components/ui/badge";

<Badge>New</Badge>
<Badge variant="secondary">Draft</Badge>
<Badge variant="outline">Beta</Badge>
<Badge variant="destructive">Failed</Badge>`;

const statusExample = `<p>
  Order 1042 <Badge>{shipped ? "Shipped" : "Processing"}</Badge>
</p>
{/* Rendered from the start, empty, so the change is announced. */}
<p role="status">{message}</p>`;
---

<ComponentPage slug="badge">
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    It is a <code>&lt;span&gt;</code>, read inline with the text around it.
  </Fragment>

  <BadgeDemo slot="demo" client:visible />

  <Fragment slot="usage">
    <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">Usage</h2>
    <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm [&_pre]:p-4">
      <Code code={usageExample} lang="tsx" theme={codeTheme} />
    </div>
    <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-muted-foreground">
      <li>
        <strong>The text is the meaning, never color alone.</strong> "Failed" in red still says
        "Failed" to someone who cannot tell red from gray, and to a screen reader. An icon can
        repeat the text; hide it with <code>aria-hidden</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Not interactive.</strong> A badge is not focusable and has no role. For something
        people can act on, such as removing a filter, use a Button or a Link instead.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Variants.</strong> <code>default</code> (primary), <code>secondary</code>,
        <code>outline</code> and <code>destructive</code>. In forced-colors mode the fill is dropped
        and the border keeps the shape.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-10 font-display text-xl">A status that changes</h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
      Screen readers do not announce a badge whose text changes. Announce the change from a
      <code>role="status"</code> region that is already on the page, as the demo does:
    </p>
    <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm [&_pre]:p-4">
      <Code code={statusExample} lang="tsx" theme={codeTheme} />
    </div>
  </Fragment>

  <p slot="install-notes" class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground">
    It creates <code>components/ui/badge.tsx</code>, the same path as shadcn's Badge, and exports
    <code>badgeVariants</code>. Unlike shadcn's, it has no <code>asChild</code>: a badge is never a
    link. It needs <code>class-variance-authority</code> and your <code>cn()</code> helper; if your
    <code>lib/utils.ts</code> differs from the current shadcn version, the CLI asks before replacing
    it, and answering no is safe.
  </p>
</ComponentPage>
